  /usr/local/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/quickjs/lws.so, was stale relative to
  the source tree, so the fix already present in source wasn't reflected
  in the binary being run. Rebuilt build/x86_64-linux-debug and copied
  lws.so to /usr/local/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/quickjs/lws.so and /tmp/lws.so
  (both paths qjsm resolves at runtime, per existing build-dir guidance)
  to fix. Worth remembering: a fix landing in source here does not take
  effect for ollama-repl until lws.so is rebuilt and reinstalled to both
  of those paths.

    not practically reproducible in plain JS - the mismatch was between
    the installed .so and the source tree, not a logic bug

- verbose-console-config-not-optional (lib/lws/util.js:94): verbose() calls
  console.config({...}) unconditionally, but the module-level LC constant
  right above it (line 3) guards the same call with `console.config?.(...)`
  - console.config is only defined on the richer Console the interactive
  qjsm REPL sets up, not on the plain global console present when a script
  runs via `qjsm -m some-module.js`. Any call to debug()/verbose() (e.g.
  fetch()'s own `log('rest', rest)` in lib/fetch.js) throws "TypeError: not
  a function" in that mode unless DEBUG is set to something that makes
  debug()'s default level (1) exceed debugLevel - found while testing
  gemini-client.js's chat()/chatStream() (both go through fetch()) via
  `qjsm -m`, worked around for that testing by exporting DEBUG=0.

    import { fetch } from './lib/fetch.js';
    await fetch('http://example.com'); // throws inside verbose() when run via `qjsm -m script.js`, not inside the interactive REPL

- tls-client-large-body-hangs-or-closes (lib/lws/protocols.js:264,
  onClientHttpWriteable) [FIXED]: a POST body over TLS (an https://
  clientConnect) as small as ~5-7KB intermittently either hung forever or
  dropped the connection immediately with a bare "closed" error - same
  request size against the same code path was NOT deterministic: 1000 and
  4000 bytes reliably succeeded, but repeated runs at
  5000/6000/7000/8000/12000/16000/20000/28000 bytes each independently hit
  either the hang or the close, with no clear size threshold separating
  them. Root cause: onClientHttpWriteable used to call `wsi.write(session.
  body.buffer, LWS_WRITE_HTTP_FINAL)` unconditionally every time it fired,
  on the assumption (stated in its own old comment) that it only ever
  fires once per body. False for a body that doesn't fully drain from
  wsi.write()'s internal write queue in one round - lws-socket.c's
  socket_flush() re-arms LWS_CALLBACK_CLIENT_HTTP_WRITEABLE to finish
  draining the remainder at the native level (confirmed by reading
  lws-protocol.c's dispatch: `is_writeable_reason(reason)` always runs
  socket_flush() first, then still falls through to call the JS-level
  `on<Reason>` handler again on the *same* re-armed callback, with nothing
  suppressing that second JS call) - and that re-entrant call used to
  `wsi.write()` the *entire original body* a second time, queuing extra
  duplicate bytes past what Content-Length had already promised and
  corrupting the request framing. A raw TCP write (the Ollama path, no
  TLS) almost always drains a body this size in one shot, which is why
  this never surfaced there - lws_write() over TLS is bounded by the TLS
  record size and legitimately returns a partial write far more often.
  Fixed by setting `session.body = null` right after the (one real) queue
  call, so a spurious re-entrant call hits the existing `!session.body`
  guard and becomes a no-op - the native retry/re-arm in socket_flush()
  was already sufficient to drain the rest on its own; the JS handler just
  needed to stop re-queuing on top of it. Verified fixed: repeated calls
  at 4000-16000 bytes that used to intermittently hang/close now
  consistently return a real, well-formed HTTP response (a 429 rate-limit
  JSON body, once the free-tier daily quota got used up during testing -
  a corrupted/duplicated body would much more likely produce a raw
  connection error or a 400, not a clean structured JSON error).
  Not fully reverified at 20-28KB specifically: testing hit Gemini's
  free-tier daily quota (20 requests/day/model) partway through, and
  "closed" errors seen there afterward are plausibly just quota
  exhaustion manifesting as a connection reset rather than a residual
  version of this bug - re-run the repro below (after the daily quota
  resets) at a few sizes in that range to be sure before assuming it's
  100% clear.

    import { GeminiClient } from './examples/ollama-repl/lib/gemini-client.js';
    const client = new GeminiClient({});
    await client.chat([{ role: 'system', content: 'x'.repeat(6000) }, { role: 'user', content: 'hi' }]);
    // used to intermittently hang forever or reject with "Gemini connection failed: closed" -
    // now returns a normal response (or a clean HTTP error) every time

- stray-single-char-prefix-dirs (qjs-lws/): four empty directories - u/,
  ut/, uti/, util/ - sit alongside the legitimate utils/ directory in the
  project root, all with the identical mtime (2026-08-04 23:29). Never
  showed up in `git status` (git doesn't track empty directories), so
  they'd been sitting there unnoticed - found while filtering
  gatherProjectContext()'s (repl.js) top-level layout listing down to
  what .gitignore allows. Looks like something created utils/ by writing
  its path one character at a time (mkdir 'u', then 'ut', then 'uti',
  then 'util', then finally the real 'utils') and left every intermediate
  step behind instead of just the final directory - same shape as the
  character-by-character terminal-echo behavior seen in ChatREPL's own
  output (examples/ollama-repl/lib/chat-repl.js) during manual testing
  elsewhere in this session, though nothing connects them directly yet.
  Not fixed or removed here - pre-existing, unrelated to the change in
  progress.

    not reproducible in plain JS - filesystem-state oddity, not a code
    path caught in the act; `ls -la qjs-lws/u* qjs-lws/util` shows all
    four as empty with matching mtimes
